# ============================================================
# .env.example — Environment variables for the go-boilerplate stack
# Copy to .env and adjust values for your environment.
# All vars shown with their local/compose defaults.
#
# SECURITY NOTE — reading secrets from files (production pattern):
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Two mechanisms keep raw credentials out of the process environment;
# both apply to config.Secret fields. See docs/operations.md §Secrets
# sourcing for the full mapping (Docker secrets, external-secrets, SOPS).
#
# 1. <NAME>_FILE convention (PREFERRED, esp. on Kubernetes) — built into
#    platform/config.Load: when NAME is unset but NAME_FILE is set, the
#    secret is read from that file (trailing newline trimmed). No struct
#    tag changes; works for every config.Secret field, nested included.
#    Precedence: NAME env var > NAME_FILE > envDefault.
#
#      PG_DSN_FILE=/run/secrets/pg_dsn        (Docker secret mount)
#      S3_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/var/run/secrets/app/s3-key   (K8s volume)
#
# 2. caarlos0/env `,file` struct-tag modifier — the env var itself holds
#    a PATH and the library loads the file's contents as the value:
#
#      PGPassword string `env:"PG_PASSWORD,file"` // PG_PASSWORD holds a path
#      PG_PASSWORD=/run/secrets/pg_password
#
#    Works, but changes the variable's meaning for every environment, so
#    the boilerplate's own configs use the _FILE convention instead.
#
# The variables below marked "SECRET" should be provided via mounted
# files in production. The .env values here are insecure defaults for
# local dev only.
#
# In Go these fields are typed config.Secret (platform/config): they print
# [REDACTED] in logs and %v dumps, and the raw value is only accessible
# via an explicit .Reveal() call — `git grep "\.Reveal()"` audits every
# place a secret leaves the config layer.
# ============================================================


# ── Deployment profile ───────────────────────────────────────
# APP_ENV=production activates the startup safety preflight (W1.2): the gateway
# REFUSES to boot when auth is disabled, PG_DSN is unencrypted/defaulted, S3 is
# plaintext against a remote endpoint, CORS is "*", AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_JWKS is
# true or GATEWAY_JWKS_URL is non-https, or credentials would cross the network
# in cleartext (KAFKA SASL without TLS / REDIS_PASSWORD without TLS). Unset or
# any other value (development/staging/test) keeps the convenient local defaults.
APP_ENV=development
# Escape hatch for the cleartext-credential preflight: set true ONLY when Kafka/
# Redis traffic rides a trusted private network (mTLS sidecar, VPC, service mesh)
# and you accept plaintext SASL/AUTH passwords on the wire. Default false.
#APP_ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_TRANSPORT=false


# ── Logging (log.Config) ─────────────────────────────────────
# LOG_LEVEL: debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOG_FORMAT: json | console
LOG_FORMAT=json


# ── Postgres (pg.Config) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Primary (writer) DSN. Each service uses its own database:
#   gateway / orders / payments / notifications
# SECRET: in production mount the DSN as a file (see header note);
# the DSN embeds the DB password — never commit a production DSN.
PG_DSN=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/gateway?sslmode=disable
# Optional read replica DSN. Leave empty to use the writer pool.
PG_READER_DSN=
# Optional separate DSN for migrations (higher-privilege role). Empty = PG_DSN.
PG_MIGRATE_URL=
# Tier-3 horizontal Postgres sharding (pg.ShardedPool, ADR-0019). EMPTY = single
# pool, M=1 (this service uses PG_DSN — byte-identical to a plain pool, the
# default). To shard, set a comma-separated DSN list of THIS service's database
# on each physical shard; aggregates route by order_id (FNV-1a, the Kafka record
# key) so M shards give ~M× write capacity. Under sharding leave PG_MIGRATE_URL
# UNSET — NewSharded rejects it at M>1 (each shard migrates via its own DSN).
# See docs/operations.md § "Tier-3 — Postgres sharding" and docker-compose.shard.yml.
#   PG_SHARDS=postgres://app:app@pg-0:5432/orders?sslmode=disable,postgres://app:app@pg-1:5432/orders?sslmode=disable
PG_SHARDS=
# Optional matching reader DSN list (reader/writer split per shard). Empty =
# inherit PG_READER_DSN (or the writer when no replica is set).
PG_READER_SHARDS=
# Privileged DSN for audit-log retention. audit_log is append-only for the app
# role (UPDATE/DELETE revoked by migration 00003), so the retention cleaner
# must connect as a role that retains DELETE (deploy/postgres/init.sql:
# audit_admin). Empty = retention disabled; rows accumulate but can never be
# rewritten/erased through the app connection.
#   PG_AUDIT_ADMIN_URL=postgres://audit_admin:audit_admin@localhost:5432/gateway?sslmode=disable
PG_AUDIT_ADMIN_URL=
# Connection pool tuning (optional — defaults are sensible for dev).
PG_MAX_CONNS=10
PG_MIN_CONNS=2
PG_MAX_CONN_LIFETIME=30m
PG_MAX_CONN_IDLE_TIME=5m
PG_HEALTH_CHECK_PERIOD=1m
# Reader pool sizing; 0 = inherit PG_MAX_CONNS / PG_MIN_CONNS.
PG_READER_MAX_CONNS=0
PG_READER_MIN_CONNS=0
# Per-connection-attempt dial timeout.
PG_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5s
# Server-side statement_timeout applied to every pooled connection.
PG_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=30s
# Prepared-statement cache mode: 0 = default (prepared), see pg.StatementCacheMode.
PG_STATEMENT_CACHE_MODE=0
# pg.query.duration histogram per query/pool (pgx tracer). Default on.
PG_QUERY_METRICS=true

# ── Optional: PgBouncer in front of Postgres ─────────────────
# Compose profile "pgbouncer" (docker compose --profile pgbouncer up -d
# postgres pgbouncer) starts PgBouncer in TRANSACTION pooling mode on host
# port 6432, wildcard-routed to all four service databases. Uncomment the
# three vars below (per service DB) to route the app through it:
#
# 1. DSN through PgBouncer (transaction pooling).
#PG_DSN=postgres://app:app@localhost:6432/gateway?sslmode=disable
# 2. pgx's default prepared-statement plans don't survive transaction
#    pooling — switch to describe-exec (pg.StatementCacheModeDescribeExec).
#PG_STATEMENT_CACHE_MODE=1
# 3. REQUIRED: migrations dial Postgres DIRECTLY (port 5432, never through
#    PgBouncer) — the migration runner holds a session advisory lock on one
#    connection, which transaction pooling breaks. See docs/operations.md
#    § Migration strategies and pg.Config.MigrateURL.
#PG_MIGRATE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/gateway?sslmode=disable


# ── Kafka / Redpanda (kafka.Config) ──────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of Kafka seed brokers.
KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:19092
# Human-readable client ID logged by brokers.
KAFKA_CLIENT_ID=app
# Consumer group ID — overridden per-consumer inside each service.
KAFKA_GROUP_ID=
# Transport security (ADR-0014). Dev defaults: no SASL, no TLS (plaintext).
# Mechanism: empty=none | PLAIN | SCRAM-SHA-256 | SCRAM-SHA-512.
#KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM=
#KAFKA_SASL_USER=
#KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD=
#KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=false
# DEV ONLY: disables broker cert verification (MITM-able). Never true in prod.
#KAFKA_TLS_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY=false
# Confluent Schema Registry URL (Redpanda ships one on :8081). When set,
# outbox-published record values use the Confluent wire format and consumers
# decode it; producers and consumers of a topic must agree (set on both or
# neither). Unset = raw protobuf (default — runnable without a registry).
#SERDE_SR_URL=http://localhost:8081


# ── Servicekit harness (servicekit.Config — all services) ────
# Admin HTTP server: /livez, /readyz, /metrics.
ADMIN_HTTP_ADDR=:9090
# false = a failed admin bind is fatal (pod must not come up half-alive).
ADMIN_BIND_OPTIONAL=false
# Apply embedded migrations on startup. Production: run a dedicated
# migrate job pre-deploy and set false so replicas never race a migration.
MIGRATE_ON_START=true
# Continuous profiling: set e.g. http://pyroscope:4040 to enable; empty = off.
PYROSCOPE_ADDR=
# How long /readyz stays 503 before teardown begins (LB drain window).
DRAIN_GRACE=5s
# Topic auto-provisioning (Service.EnsureTopics). Production: manage
# topics as IaC and set ENSURE_TOPICS=false; run RF >= 3.
ENSURE_TOPICS=true
TOPIC_PARTITIONS=6
TOPIC_RF=1
TOPIC_RETENTION=168h
# In-process consumer retry (AddConsumer's WithRetry wrap: blocking
# attempts, then DLT). Tiered retry is configured in code via retry.Policy.
CONSUMER_RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
CONSUMER_RETRY_BACKOFF=100ms
# Inbox dedup rows older than INBOX_RETENTION are deleted every
# INBOX_CLEANUP_INTERVAL. INBOX_RETENTION MUST be >= TOPIC_RETENTION
# (replay-after-cleanup defense): with APP_ENV=production the service REFUSES to
# start when it is shorter; outside production it only warns.
INBOX_RETENTION=168h
INBOX_CLEANUP_INTERVAL=1h
# Audit rows older than AUDIT_RETENTION (default 90 days) are deleted every
# AUDIT_CLEANUP_INTERVAL. Review compliance obligations before lowering.
AUDIT_RETENTION=2160h
AUDIT_CLEANUP_INTERVAL=6h
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY keys the audit hash chain with HMAC-SHA256 instead of plain
# sha256. UNSET (default) = keyless: detects edit/delete of existing rows
# (tamper-evidence) but the app role can still FORGE rows by append. SET (ideally
# from a secret manager, NOT readable by the app's DB role) = forgery-resistant
# against a compromised app. Keep it stable: rotating it invalidates VerifyChain
# over rows hashed with the old key.
#AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY=
# AUDIT_CHAIN_SHARDS splits the audit hash chain into N actor-keyed chains
# (ADR-0018) so commands by different actors stop serializing on one chain-head
# lock. 1 = single global chain (default, unchanged). Raise toward the number of
# distinct hot actors when the audit chain lock caps command throughput. A given
# actor always maps to one chain, so per-actor audit order is preserved.
AUDIT_CHAIN_SHARDS=1
# Async-audit tuning (Eventual-consistency flows only — A2):
# BufferedAuditWriter is configured at wiring time in Go code, not via env vars.
# Adjust audit.WriterConfig{Buffer, BatchSize, FlushInterval} in your service's
# wiring (defaults: 4096 buffer / 128 batch / 100ms flush). Register the writer
# before servicekit.Start via svc.AddAuditWriter(writer).
# Monitor these two metrics and alert when non-zero:
#   audit.dropped_total             — buffer full; entries dropped (audit gap)
#   audit.async_write_errors_total  — flush failure (DB error on background write)


# ── HTTP server (httpserver.Config — gateway only) ───────────
HTTP_ADDR=:8080
HTTP_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT=5s
HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT=15s
HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=60s
HTTP_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576
HTTP_HANDLER_TIMEOUT=30s
# Strict-Transport-Security max-age in seconds emitted by SecurityHeaders
# (default 31536000 = 1 year, with includeSubDomains). Only meaningful when TLS
# terminates at or before this server. Set 0 to disable HSTS — choose that when
# a TLS-terminating ingress/load balancer owns the header instead.
HTTP_HSTS_MAX_AGE=31536000


# ── OpenTelemetry (telemetry.Config) ─────────────────────────
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=gateway
# gRPC endpoint of the OTel Collector (host:port, no scheme).
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=localhost:4317
# Set to true to enable OTLP trace+metric export; false = no-op tracer.
OTEL_ENABLED=false
# Prometheus-pull /metrics reader — stays on even when OTEL_ENABLED=false.
OTEL_METRICS_PROMETHEUS=true
# Duration histograms aggregate as base-2 exponential (Prometheus NATIVE
# histograms downstream) by default. Set true to fall back to per-signal
# tuned explicit buckets for backends without native-histogram support
# (docs/operations.md §Latency tails).
TELEMETRY_CLASSIC_HISTOGRAMS=false
# Head sampling ratio for traces: 1.0 = sample all, 0.0 = none.
TELEMETRY_TRACE_RATIO=1.0
# true = also export structured logs via OTLP (stdout+collector fan-out).
TELEMETRY_LOGS=false


# ── Redis / two-tier cache (cache.Config) ────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of Redis addresses.
REDIS_ADDRS=localhost:6379
# Transport security (ADR-0014). Applies to the cache, rate-limit and SSE
# rueidis clients. Dev defaults: no password, no TLS.
#REDIS_PASSWORD=
#REDIS_TLS_ENABLED=false
# Max entries in the in-process L1 cache.
CACHE_L1_CAPACITY=10000
# TTL used when the caller does not supply one.
CACHE_DEFAULT_TTL=5m
# Fractional TTL jitter (0.1 = ±10%) to spread expiry load.
CACHE_TTL_JITTER=0.1
# Pub/sub invalidation channel suffix ("cache:inv:<prefix>") — one L1
# coherence domain per prefix; give each service its own.
CACHE_INV_PREFIX=default
# Upper bound on a GetOrLoad loader call (its only cancellation source).
CACHE_LOADER_TIMEOUT=10s
# Per-operation timeout for L2 (Redis) calls; failures feed the L2 breaker.
CACHE_L2_OP_TIMEOUT=1s


# ── S3 / SeaweedFS (blob.Config) ─────────────────────────────
# SECRET: in production mount S3_SECRET_KEY / S3_ACCESS_KEY as files
# (see header note) instead of plain env values.
# Local dev creds match deploy/seaweedfs/s3.json (docker-compose seaweedfs).
S3_ENDPOINT=localhost:8333
S3_ACCESS_KEY=seaweedadmin
S3_SECRET_KEY=seaweedadmin
S3_BUCKET=app
S3_USE_SSL=false
S3_REGION=us-east-1
# Path-style addressing (http://host:port/bucket/key) — required for local
# S3-compatible stores (SeaweedFS, LocalStack); set false for AWS S3.
S3_USE_PATH_STYLE=true


# ── Gateway auth (gateway.Config) ────────────────────────────
# Set to true ONLY for local dev/demo — all endpoints become open.
GATEWAY_AUTH_DISABLED=true
# Keycloak JWKS URL (used when GATEWAY_AUTH_DISABLED=false).
GATEWAY_JWKS_URL=http://localhost:8180/realms/app/protocol/openid-connect/certs
GATEWAY_JWKS_ISSUER=http://localhost:8180/realms/app
GATEWAY_JWKS_AUDIENCE=gateway
# The verifier refuses a non-https JWKS URL (ADR-0014). Dev uses the http
# Keycloak above, so opt in — DEV ONLY. In prod point GATEWAY_JWKS_URL at
# https and leave this false (an http JWKS can be MITM-swapped to forge tokens).
#AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_JWKS=false
# Acceptable clock skew for exp/iat/nbf validation (issuer/verifier drift).
AUTH_CLOCK_SKEW=30s
# When set, the token's azp claim must match this OAuth client id. Empty = off.
AUTH_REQUIRED_AZP=
# Max Bearer-token size (bytes) the auth middleware will verify. A larger token
# is rejected with 401 by a cheap len-check BEFORE jwt.Parse, so an oversized
# Authorization header cannot force per-request parse work. Default 8192.
AUTH_MAX_TOKEN_BYTES=8192


# ── Gateway edge (CORS, rate limit, projection) ──────────────
# Comma-separated allowed CORS origins. Empty = deny all cross-origin
# browser requests. "*" for local dev only — never with credentials.
GATEWAY_CORS_ORIGINS=
# Per-client-IP token bucket: sustained refill rate and burst depth.
RATELIMIT_RPS=50
RATELIMIT_BURST=100
# Authed-tier limiter keyed per principal (token subject; anonymous falls
# back to client IP), chained AFTER the per-IP tier. 0 disables.
RATELIMIT_AUTHED_RPS=200
RATELIMIT_AUTHED_BURST=400
# true = distributed Redis-backed limiters — per-IP AND authed tier (falls
# back to in-memory if Redis is unavailable); false = in-memory limiters.
RATELIMIT_REDIS=false
# Behaviour of the Redis-backed limiters when Redis is unavailable.
# false (default) = FAIL OPEN: admit requests during a Redis outage (preserves
# availability, but can admit bursts beyond the limit). true = FAIL CLOSED:
# deny requests while Redis is down (strict enforcement; sheds load). The
# production preflight REQUIRES true when RATELIMIT_REDIS=true. Redis errors
# always bump the ratelimit.errors counter + log a WARN regardless of this.
RATELIMIT_FAIL_CLOSED=false
# Comma-separated CIDR prefixes whose X-Forwarded-For is trusted for
# client-IP extraction. Empty = use RemoteAddr only.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=
# true = gateway also runs the read-model projection consumer in-process
# (single-binary demo). false = projection runs as its own deployment.
GATEWAY_EMBEDDED_PROJECTION=true
# true = projection applies each partition's poll records in ONE transaction
# (one tx per partition-batch-per-poll — higher throughput). false = one tx per
# event. Same dedup/ordering/hooks/DLT contract either way (a DLT from the
# projection is alerted as a code/schema bug, not bad input).
GATEWAY_PROJECTION_BATCH=true
# SSE (GET /v1/orders/{id}/events): keep-alive comment interval — keep it
# below any load balancer's idle-connection timeout.
GATEWAY_SSE_HEARTBEAT=15s
# SSE fallback polling cadence of the projection store when REDIS_ADDRS
# is unset/unreachable (no pub/sub push available).
GATEWAY_SSE_POLL_INTERVAL=2s
# Cap on concurrently open SSE streams per replica; new streams beyond it
# get 503 GATEWAY_SSE_SATURATED. Default 4096 is a safe per-replica bulkhead;
# set 0 to explicitly opt OUT (unlimited).
GATEWAY_SSE_MAX_STREAMS=4096
# true = batch pending-row inserts on POST /v1/orders (higher write throughput
# under burst load at the cost of a small async window before reads see the row).
GATEWAY_PENDING_ASYNC=false
# Upload Content-Type allowlist for order attachments. An upload whose media
# type is not listed is rejected with 415 GATEWAY_ATTACHMENT_TYPE_REJECTED —
# defence-in-depth against stored XSS (renderable types such as text/html and
# image/svg+xml are deliberately absent; Download also forces an attachment
# disposition). Comma-separated; empty falls back to the same default set.
GATEWAY_ATTACHMENT_CONTENT_TYPES=application/pdf,image/png,image/jpeg,image/gif,text/plain,application/octet-stream


# ── Kafka topics ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rule: a topic env is named after the TOPIC it points at, never after a
# service (no GATEWAY_/SHIPPING_ prefixes). Several services read the same
# env name — each container has its own env namespace, so "same topic =
# same env name" holds by construction.
# orders.commands: gateway produces, orders consumes.
ORDERS_COMMANDS_TOPIC=orders.commands
# orders.events: orders produces, payments + gateway projection consume.
ORDERS_EVENTS_TOPIC=orders.events
# payments.events: payments produces; orders, notifications + gateway
# projection consume.
PAYMENTS_EVENTS_TOPIC=payments.events


# ── Orders service ────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long an order may stay unpaid before it is auto-cancelled.
ORDERS_PAYMENT_DEADLINE=15m
# How often the unpaid-order watcher scans for expired orders.
ORDERS_UNPAID_CHECK_INTERVAL=1m


# ── Outbox relay (outbox.RelayConfig) ─────────────────────────
OUTBOX_BATCH_SIZE=100
OUTBOX_POLL_INTERVAL=1s
# Published outbox rows older than this are deleted by the cleaner.
OUTBOX_RETENTION_AGE=24h
OUTBOX_CLEANUP_INTERVAL=1h
# true = pg advisory lock makes one relay instance active at a time.
OUTBOX_SINGLE_ACTIVE=true
# Relay sharding (ADR-0017): 1 = single relay (default). N > 1 = N independently
# leader-elected shard relays (aggregates hashed to shards) → ~N× publish
# throughput WITH per-aggregate order preserved. Set N ≤ service instance count.
OUTBOX_RELAY_SHARDS=1

# ── Outbox partitioning (outbox.PartitionConfig, ADR-0016) ────
# simple (default): every row lands in the DEFAULT partition; the age-based
#   cleaner above is the retention mechanism. No maintenance worker runs.
# partitioned: a leader-elected worker pre-creates time-range partitions and
#   DETACH+DROPs expired ones (never one holding unpublished rows). The table is
#   RANGE-partitioned by created_at from day one, so flipping this is NOT a
#   migration — just set the mode and wire AddOutboxPartitionMaintenance.
OUTBOX_PARTITION_MODE=simple
OUTBOX_PARTITION_INTERVAL=720h
OUTBOX_PARTITION_RETENTION=2160h
OUTBOX_PARTITION_LOOKAHEAD=2


# ── Go runtime tuning ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# GOMEMLIMIT sets a soft heap limit so the GC trims memory before the
# Linux cgroup OOM-killer fires.  Set to ~90% of the container's hard
# memory limit (deploy.resources.limits.memory in docker-compose /
# resources.limits.memory in Kubernetes).
#
# Example: container limit = 512 MiB  →  GOMEMLIMIT = 460MiB (≈90%)
# Example: container limit = 1 GiB    →  GOMEMLIMIT = 920MiB
# Example: container limit = 2 GiB    →  GOMEMLIMIT = 1800MiB
#
# The docker-compose.yml services already set GOMEMLIMIT=460MiB to match
# their deploy.resources.limits.memory=512M.  Update both together.
#
# Units: B, KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB (no spaces around the value).
GOMEMLIMIT=460MiB
#
# GOMAXPROCS is set automatically at startup via go.uber.org/automaxprocs,
# which reads the container CPU quota from the Linux cgroup.  Go 1.25+
# also does this natively when GOMAXPROCS is unset, so automaxprocs is
# belt-and-suspenders.  Only set this to override automaxprocs explicitly.
#GOMAXPROCS=2
#
# Use whole-number CPU limits (cpus: "1.0", "2.0", …) in deploy.resources
# to avoid CFS throttling artefacts from fractional quotas.
# Each service runs in its own container and therefore has its own env
# namespace — no per-service env-prefix is needed.  To run multiple
# services in a single process, pass distinct configs explicitly (as the
# e2e test does) rather than relying on env variables.
